<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>A comparison of <span id="S1.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> and our unified evaluation framework with prior works.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Synthetic data generation offers a scalable privacy-preserving alternative. Early efforts focused on single-turn counseling data generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Later, methods were developed to generate multi-turn counseling dialogues from single-turn psychological Q&amp;A data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al. 2024</a>)</cite> or using role-playing LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu and Lan 2024</a>)</cite>. However, these approaches lack grounding in therapeutic theory and rely on a single LLM agent, failing to reflect real-world counseling strategies. To improve this limitation, CPsycoun <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> uses counseling memos, and CACTUS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite> integrates a Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) based planning agent, but both still use a single agent to produce counselor responses. As a result, they fall short in modeling therapeutic strategies such as reflection, questioning, solution provision, normalization, and psycho-education <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. To address these limitations, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>, a novel multi-agent framework that decomposes counselor response generation into coordinated sub-tasks handled by five specialized response agents each grounded in core therapeutic techniques – reflection, questioning, solution provision, normalization, and psycho-education and a final response generation agent that composes a coherent, context-aware counselor utterance guided by a dynamic technique selector agent and a CBT-based planning agent. On the client side, we simulate realistic behavior using detailed profiles and attitude modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another persistent challenge in this domain is the lack of standardized evaluation. Different studies adopt different metrics, making it difficult to meaningfully compare the effectiveness of synthetic data and fine-tuned models. For instance, CACTUS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite> uses measures like Positive and Negative Affect Schedule (PANAS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Watson, Clark, and Tellegen 1988</a>)</cite> and Cognitive Therapy Rating Scale (CTRS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aarons et al. 2012</a>)</cite>, while other works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu and Lan 2024</a>)</cite> use Working Alliance Inventory (WAI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Horvath and Greenberg 1989</a>)</cite>. A similar pattern is observed in expert evaluation as well. To address this, we introduce a unified evaluation framework that consolidates evaluation metrics from prior works alongside expanding expert evaluation from four to nine counseling aspects, enabling more rigorous and comprehensive assessment of synthetic data and fine-tuned models. Through this comprehensive evaluation, we demonstrate that <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> outperforms the current state-of-the-art methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>)</cite> by <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.2</annotation></semantics></math>% on general counseling skills and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.3</annotation></semantics></math>% on CBT-specific skills on average on CTRS for the generated counseling sessions. Furthermore, a Llama3-8B-Instruction model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta 2024</a>)</cite> fine-tuned on <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>-generated data exhibits performance gains of <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.3</annotation></semantics></math>% on general counseling skills and <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.3</annotation></semantics></math>% on CBT-specific skills on average on CTRS over those fine-tuned with data generated by baseline methods. Crucially, <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>-generated sessions are preferred by human experts in <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>77.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.2</annotation></semantics></math>% of cases on average across nine aspects when compared to those produced by the strongest baseline. Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows a comparison of our study to existing ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions include:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>, a novel psychologically grounded multi-agent synthetic counseling session generation framework.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune an open-source model on data generated by <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>, achieving strong gains in counseling metrics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a unified evaluation framework that consolidates evaluations from prior work and expands expert assessment to nine aspects of counseling.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Synthetic Counseling Data Generation</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to privacy constraints limiting access to real counseling data, there is growing interest in synthetic counseling dialogue generation. Psych8k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>)</cite> introduced a framework that generates counselor responses to client questions. However, it is limited to single-turn interactions. To address the need for multi-turn conversations, subsequent works such as SMILE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and SoulChat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2023</a>)</cite> convert single-turn psychological Q&amp;A data into multi-turn dialogues. Another line of work uses two LLMs in a role-play setup (one acting as the client and the other as the counselor) to simulate counseling interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu and Lan 2024</a>)</cite>. However, these methods lack grounding in psychology theory. To improve psychological grounding, CPsyCoun <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> generates multi-turn sessions from counseling memos, while CACTUS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite> incorporates CBT-based planning. However, counselors in high-quality counseling sessions respond using a combination of various psychotherapeutic techniques like reflection, questioning, solution provision, normalization, and psycho-education <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, which cannot be realized using a single LLM like CPsyCoun and CACTUS. In contrast, <span id="S2.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> decomposes response generation across specialized agents, each aligned with a therapeutic technique, coordinated by a technique selector and a CBT planning agent, followed by a response generation agent that generates the final counselor response, thereby breaking down the generation process into manageable sub-tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Multi-Agent Framework</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs often struggle with executing complex tasks in isolation. Multi-agent frameworks address this limitation by decomposing a complex task into simpler sub-tasks distributed across collaborating agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, showing effectiveness in domains such as recommender systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and task-oriented dialogue systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Counselor response generation is similarly complex, requiring a nuanced understanding of client issues and the application of multiple therapeutic techniques such as reflection, questioning, problem solving, normalization, and psycho-education <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. However, prior works simulate the counselor using a single LLM. In contrast, <span id="S2.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> models the counselor as a multi-agent framework with specialized agents, each grounded in distinct therapeutic techniques alongside a CBT planning agent, a technique selection agent, and a response generation agent. This design enables a psychologically grounded generation process that better captures the structure and dynamics of real counseling sessions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04183v1/MASCS-Gen-5.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/434;" width="698" height="434" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S2.F1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overview of <span id="S2.F1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>.</span> Counselor response is generated using specialized response agents (reflection, questioning, solutions, normalizing, psycho-education), a technique agent, a CBT agent, and a response generation agent.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Our Proposed Model</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>, a novel multi-agent framework for synthetic counseling session generation that explicitly models the complex, psychologically grounded reasoning processes involved in counseling. Unlike prior role-play based approaches that rely on a single LLM agent to simulate counselor responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu and Lan 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> decomposes the response generation process into modular, specialized agents, each responsible for a key counseling function. This design enables finer control and better alignment with established therapeutic practices in generated dialogues. Similar to prior LLM-based simulations, <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> adopts a two-party role-play paradigm where LLMs simulate both counselor and client roles. However, we move beyond prior work by using a multi-agent system for counselor simulation, enabling the explicit modeling of distinct psychological techniques such as reflection, questioning, solution provision, normalization, and psycho-education <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. To generate realistic and diverse interactions, we initialize each session using detailed client intake forms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, which contain information such as client background, client issues, and reasons for seeking therapy. Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Multi-Agent Framework ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents a schematic diagram of <span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>. The remaining section describes the multi-agent counselor simulation and the client simulation in detail.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Multi-Agent Counselor Simulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective counseling responses are therapeutically nuanced, requiring both a structured treatment plan and dynamic use of psychological techniques. To mirror this complexity, <span id="S3.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> simulates the counselor using a coordinated ensemble of LLM agents – (i) a CBT agent to produce a structured treatment plan, (ii) five specialized response agents, each focusing on a specific psychological technique, (iii) a technique agent to determine the appropriate combination of techniques for a given turn, and (iv) a response generation agent to synthesize the final counselor response. Now we will describe each agent in detail (see Appendix A for the details of the prompt used for each agent).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
