</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines and Ablations.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> with two state-of-the-art synthetic counseling data generation pipelines: Psych8k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>)</cite> and CACTUS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. We use <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math> CACTUS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite> client profiles with <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> attitude variations each resulting in <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="450" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>450</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">450</annotation></semantics></math> initial generation seeds to generate 40-turn counseling dialogues per method. The prompt details for counselor and client simulation are provided in Appendix A and Appendix B, respectively. To assess real-world utility, we fine-tune a Llama3-8B-Instruct model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta 2024</a>)</cite> on each dataset, resulting in Llama-Psych8k, Llama-CACTUS, and Llama-<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>. We further conduct ablation studies to isolate and understand the contribution of individual agents in <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>. Specifically, we evaluate three ablations: <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">-C</span></sub></span> without the CBT agent, <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">-T</span></sub></span> without the technique agent, and <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">-C-T</span></sub></span> with no CBT and technique agent. The ablation prompts details are provided in Appendix H.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLMs Used.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Llama3-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta 2024</a>)</cite> as the primary backbone for both data generation and fine-tuning tasks. For <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>, we use Llama3-8B-Instruct to implement the CBT agent, all specialized response agents, and the response generation agent. The technique agent uses GPT-4o-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2024a</a>)</cite> for its stronger reasoning. For both Psych8k and CACTUS, the counselor is simulated using Llama3-8B-Instruct. The client agent across all methods is implemented using Llama3-8B-Instruct to ensure uniformity. For LLM-as-a-judge evaluation, we use GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2024b</a>)</cite> as the judge model. More experimental details are provided in Appendix I.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We comprehensively evaluate <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>-generated counseling sessions on data diversity, data quality, downstream effectiveness, and expert preference. Our results show that <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> generates richer and more psychologically grounded counseling sessions that improve the training of downstream counseling agents and are highly preferred by human experts across multiple counseling aspects. We further provide example comparisons in Appendix G.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Diversity.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Multi-Agent Counselor Simulation ‣ 3 Our Proposed Model ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the Distinct-<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> scores (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>) and EAD for datasets generated by <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>  Psych8k and CACTUS. <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>  consistently achieves the highest scores across all diversity metrics, highlighting its ability to produce lexically varied counseling dialogues. Crucially, the improved EAD score, which adjusts for sequence length bias, demonstrates that this variation stems from genuine structural richness rather than shorter outputs. This confirms that our multi-agent generation paradigm encourages nuanced, context-sensitive conversations, moving beyond the repetitive patterns observed in prior methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Quality.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we evaluate the quality of synthetic counseling sessions using three established clinical metrics: CTRS, WAI, and PANAS. As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Client Simulation ‣ 3 Our Proposed Model ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> outperforms all baselines on both general and CBT-specific counseling skills. While CACTUS also integrates CBT planning, its reliance on a single-agent generation paradigm results in shallower implementation of CBT principles. In contrast, <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>’s multi-agent design featuring specialized response agents and a technique selector yields consistently higher scores across all six CTRS subcategories. On the other hand, Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Client Simulation ‣ 3 Our Proposed Model ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reveals <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>’s superiority in fostering client-counselor alliance. Our framework achieves the highest scores in Goal, Task, and Bond categories, showing its ability to generate empathetic and collaborative counselor utterances. Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Client Simulation ‣ 3 Our Proposed Model ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> also shows PANAS results. <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> elicits stronger positive emotional shifts in clients with positive or neutral attitudes, improving positive emotions more effectively than baselines. For clients with negative attitudes, however, <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> performs slightly worse. This is consistent with prior findings from CACTUS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. CACTUS suggests that models focused on deep thought exploration (via CBT and reflective techniques) may initially challenge negative attitude clients, thus showing worse performance. Standard deviations for CTRS, PANAS, and WAI across sessions are shown in Appendix (Tables 9 and 10).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Counseling Agent Fine-tuning.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess real-world utility, we fine-tune Llama3-8B-Instruct on the synthetic datasets and evaluate the resulting models using CTRS, WAI, and PANAS. Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Client Simulation ‣ 3 Our Proposed Model ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that Llama-<span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> significantly outperforms Llama-Psych8k and Llama-CACTUS across all counseling skills, indicating that <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> generates effective training data. As seen in Table <a href="#S3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Client Simulation ‣ 3 Our Proposed Model ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, Llama-<span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> also yields a stronger working alliance with clients. Similarly, PANAS results in Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Client Simulation ‣ 3 Our Proposed Model ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> mirror the trends observed in the raw data evaluation. This shows that <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> produces higher quality synthetic data suitable for fine-tuning open source LLM models for counseling tasks. Standard deviations for CTRS, PANAS, and WAI across sessions are provided in Appendix (Tables 11, 12 and 13).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Expert Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Counseling Agent Fine-tuning. ‣ 4 Unified Evaluation Framework ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that experts overwhelmingly favor (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>77.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.2</annotation></semantics></math>%) the counseling sessions generated by <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> in comparison to Psych8k across all nine aspects. This indicates that the counseling sessions generated by <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> are not only favored by automatic evaluation but also by experts. <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> demonstrates the ability to generate sessions that are not only safer (safety) and more natural (content), but also exhibit greater authenticity and professionalism, establishing a realistic and trust-building therapeutic context. Experts rate <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>-generated sessions as containing more clear (directiveness), and comprehensive (comprehensiveness) counselor responses. Furthermore, <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> sessions excel at supportive content (supportiveness) that reinforces emotional alliance, while also enhancing expressiveness, by encouraging clients to articulate their inner experiences. <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>-generated sessions also promote exploratoriness, helping clients reflect on their issues, a core aspect in counseling. We observe a similar pattern in fine-tuned models, where Llama-<span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> is highly favored by the experts over Llama-Psych8k.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ablations.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ablation results in Tables <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Expert Evaluation. ‣ 4 Unified Evaluation Framework ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Expert Evaluation. ‣ 4 Unified Evaluation Framework ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> offer valuable insights into the design of multi-agent LLM counselors. The CTRS results in Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Expert Evaluation. ‣ 4 Unified Evaluation Framework ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> show that removing the CBT agent has limited impact on general skills such as understanding and interpersonal effectiveness, as the technique agent still guides the model toward empathetic and relevant responses. However, collaboration, a skill essential for client involvement in decision-making, degrades notably, highlighting the crucial role of a structured CBT plan. For CBT-specific skills, strategy remains relatively intact without the CBT agent due to the technique agent’s informed method selection. However, Guided Discovery and Focus see significant drops. This shows the importance of the CBT agent for enhancing the CBT-specific counseling skills. In contrast, removing the technique agent has a broader effect with both general and CBT-specific counseling scores dropping significantly, underlining the necessity of selecting the right psychological techniques for crafting high-impact responses. The worst performance is seen when both agents are removed. This confirms their strong synergy in generating high-quality, psychologically grounded dialogue.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We present the WAI results in Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Expert Evaluation. ‣ 4 Unified Evaluation Framework ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Interestingly, the Task score remains largely unaffected by the ablations. This shows that CBT plan and technique selection do not affect the understanding and agreement of the client on tasks. The scores for Goal and Bond, however, reduce without the technique agent, likely due to a loss of adaptability to client needs. Removing the CBT agent shows better results on Bond. This suggests that the CBT plan, though structured, can lead to rigidity, weakening the interpersonal connection between counselor and client. This rigidity is improved by the dynamic technique selection. The lower Bond scores on removing the technique agent further support this. Removing the CBT agent also improves the score for Goal. This may appear counterintuitive since CBT gives a clear plan. From Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Expert Evaluation. ‣ 4 Unified Evaluation Framework ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we also see that using the CBT-agent leads to more collaboration with clients in goal setting. This apparent contradiction suggests a potential rigidity in the counseling process: while the CBT plan facilitates structured engagement, it may inadvertently overshadow the client’s personal preferences or evolving needs, thus leading to lower Goal scores. Similar to the Bond score, the Goal scores also reduce on removing the technique agent, showing the importance of improving the rigid counseling plan through dynamic strategy selection of the technique agent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From the PANAS results shown in Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Expert Evaluation. ‣ 4 Unified Evaluation Framework ‣ MAGneT: Coordinated Multi-Agent Generation of Synthetic Multi-Turn Mental Health Counseling Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we see that for clients with negative attitudes, removing CBT and technique agents results in better negative emotions regulation. This mirrors observations from CACTUS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and aligns with our earlier analysis: CBT-grounded techniques may inadvertently deepen emotional exploration, which, while beneficial for self-insight, may elevate negative affect in clients with negative attitude towards counseling. Standard deviations for CTRS, PANAS, and WAI across generated sessions are provided in Appendix (Tables 14 and 15).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span>, a novel multi-agent framework for synthetic counseling session generation that incorporates core psychological techniques: reflection, questioning, solution provision, normalization, and psycho-education to produce more realistic and therapeutic counselor responses. We introduce a unified evaluation framework combining CTRS, WAI, and PANAS for assessing general and CBT-specific skills, therapeutic alliance, and emotional impact. Additionally, we expand expert evaluation to nine aspects and assess downstream model effectiveness through performance of fine-tuned models. <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAGneT</span> outperforms existing methods in both data diversity and quality, as validated by automatic metrics, expert preference, and improved fine-tuned model performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
